---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ConfidenceBadge from '../components/ConfidenceBadge.astro';
import EditorialConfidenceChip from '../components/EditorialConfidenceChip.astro';
import SourceDistance from '../components/SourceDistance.astro';

// Sample source-distance chips — built inline so the page can show the
// reader the actual chip they'll encounter on claim cards across the site.
const polybiusLike = { type: 'literary', is_primary: true, temporal_distance_years: 50 };
const livyLike = { type: 'literary', is_primary: true, temporal_distance_years: 200 };
const appianLike = { type: 'literary', is_primary: true, temporal_distance_years: 300 };
const inscriptionLike = { type: 'inscription', is_primary: true };
const modernLike = { type: 'modern_scholarship', is_primary: false };
const unknownLike = { type: 'literary', is_primary: true };
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Methodology — How we know what we know"
  description="The epistemic conventions behind Qart-Hadasht: claims, confidence levels, source distance, stance, editorial takes, and open questions."
>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 py-12">
    <p class="eyebrow mb-3">Methodology</p>
    <h1 class="font-serif text-4xl sm:text-5xl mb-4">How we know what we know</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-sand-900 leading-snug">
      An encyclopedia of Carthage is, almost more than anything else, an
      argument about what the surviving evidence actually supports. The
      sources are partial, polemical, and mostly written by the city's
      enemies. Anything readers should trust on this site has to be
      visibly tied to its evidence — and visibly distinguished from
      what's inferred, contested, or unknown. This page describes how
      that machinery works.
    </p>

    <div class="prose-encyclopedia mt-10 text-sand-900">
      <h2>The unit of knowledge: the claim</h2>
      <p>
        The smallest knowable thing on the site is a <strong>claim</strong>: an
        atomic factual statement, individually citable, tagged with one
        confidence level, and backed by an explicit list of sources. "Hannibal
        deployed 80 elephants at Zama" is a claim. "The Mercenary War lasted
        from 241 to 237 BCE" is a claim. "Carthage was destroyed in 146 BCE
        and the city site was systematically demolished" is a claim. Each one
        lives in its own file and shows up wherever it's relevant.
      </p>
      <p>
        Most encyclopedia prose blurs claims together — three or four
        assertions per sentence, one citation at the end of the paragraph,
        no way to tell which sentence rests on which source. The atomic-claim
        structure is the site's protest against that. If the reader wants to
        know <em>why</em> we say something, the link is right there.
      </p>

      <h2>The confidence vocabulary</h2>
      <p>
        Every claim wears one of five confidence chips. They are the most
        important glyphs on the site:
      </p>
      <ul class="not-prose space-y-3 my-6">
        <li class="flex items-baseline gap-3 flex-wrap">
          <ConfidenceBadge confidence="attested" />
          <span class="flex-1">
            Directly stated in surviving primary sources, and not meaningfully
            disputed. The Battle of Cannae happened in 216 BCE; Hannibal
            crossed the Alps; Scipio Aemilianus oversaw the destruction in 146.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-baseline gap-3 flex-wrap">
          <ConfidenceBadge confidence="inferred" />
          <span class="flex-1">
            Reasoned from the primary evidence — not directly stated, but
            well-supported. That Cannae's double-envelopment became Hannibal's
            tactical template for Italy is not a sentence Polybius writes; it
            is a synthesis defensible from his narrative.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-baseline gap-3 flex-wrap">
          <ConfidenceBadge confidence="contested" />
          <span class="flex-1">
            Genuinely disputed in the ancient sources, between modern scholars,
            or both — with serious evidence on more than one side. The Tophet
            question lives here. So does the existence of the Philinus Treaty.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-baseline gap-3 flex-wrap">
          <ConfidenceBadge confidence="disputed" />
          <span class="flex-1">
            Active modern scholarly disagreement over a question the ancient
            sources don't directly settle. Used where the dispute is
            specifically a recent-scholarship one rather than an
            ancient-source one.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-baseline gap-3 flex-wrap">
          <ConfidenceBadge confidence="legendary" />
          <span class="flex-1">
            Drawn from legendary or mythologized tradition — Dido's flight from
            Tyre, the bull-hide founding of Carthage. Tagged this way because
            the historicity is not the point; the tradition is.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The colors are part of the design language. After a few pages they
        read faster than the words: emerald for what's solid, amber and rose
        for what's contested, sky for synthesis, stone for the legendary
        layer.
      </p>

      <h2>Sources are first-class entities</h2>
      <p>
        Every cited source — Polybius's <em>Histories</em>, Livy's <em>Ab
        Urbe Condita</em>, Diodorus's <em>Library</em>, modern monographs by
        Hoyos and Lancel and Quinn, Punic inscriptions, the archaeological
        record at Carthage and Motya — has its own page. Each source page
        shows what kind of witness the source is, when and where it was
        composed, what biases it carries, and a digest of the passages
        cited across the encyclopedia. A reader who clicks through to
        Polybius's page sees not just "Polybius" but a working table of
        contents of his usefulness for Carthaginian history: the first
        treaty at 3.22, Cannae at 3.107–117, the constitution at 6.51,
        Zama at 15.11, and so on.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is the load-bearing thing about the site. If the source layer
        works, everything else works.
      </p>

      <h2>Source distance: how close is the witness?</h2>
      <p>
        On every claim card, the cited source appears beside a
        <strong>distance chip</strong> — a small visual signal of how far the
        source sits from the events it describes. The buckets:
      </p>
      <ul class="not-prose space-y-3 my-6">
        <li class="flex items-baseline gap-3 flex-wrap">
          <SourceDistance source={polybiusLike} />
          <span class="flex-1">
            Within ~50 years. A near-contemporary witness or a writer with
            access to people who lived through the events. Polybius on the
            Punic Wars is the canonical example.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-baseline gap-3 flex-wrap">
          <SourceDistance source={livyLike} />
          <span class="flex-1">
            ~50–150 years removed. One tradition further out — oral memory
            still alive in some form, but the writer is depending on
            predecessors. Livy on the Hannibalic War sits here.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-baseline gap-3 flex-wrap">
          <SourceDistance source={appianLike} />
          <span class="flex-1">
            More than ~150 years removed. By now we are reading the literary
            tradition rather than recoverable testimony. Appian on the Punic
            Wars; Justin's epitome of Pompeius Trogus on Carthaginian
            origins.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-baseline gap-3 flex-wrap">
          <SourceDistance source={inscriptionLike} />
          <span class="flex-1">
            A material witness — inscription, coin, archaeological context —
            contemporary with the events by definition. Punic stelae from the
            Tophet, the Pyrgi tablets, foundation deposits at Motya.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-baseline gap-3 flex-wrap">
          <SourceDistance source={modernLike} />
          <span class="flex-1">
            Modern scholarship: secondary, synthetic, where the distance
            number stops being the epistemic story. Treated as a different
            kind of citation — argument and synthesis rather than testimony.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-baseline gap-3 flex-wrap">
          <SourceDistance source={unknownLike} />
          <span class="flex-1">
            Distance not recorded — typically a literary source we haven't
            yet pinned a composition date to. Worth flagging because we
            can't evaluate the witness without it.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Distance does not equal reliability — Polybius's pro-Scipionic bias
        is a real reading problem; a thousand-year-distant inscription that
        names a magistrate is closer to the truth than a contemporary's
        rumor. But the chip is the fastest way to remind a reader,
        line-by-line, that "Polybius says" and "Appian says" are not
        epistemically equivalent.
      </p>

      <h2>Access to witnesses</h2>
      <p>
        Source pages also carry an <strong>access</strong> field, distinct
        from raw distance, describing how the writer got their information:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>contemporary</strong> — direct testimony or access to
          living witnesses. Polybius on the Third Punic War (which he
          watched).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>one_step</strong> — drew on contemporaries' written
          accounts, often now lost. Diodorus on Agathocles.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>literary_tradition</strong> — working from a chain of
          earlier historians; the distance to the events runs through
          intermediate texts.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>late</strong> — substantially after the literary
          tradition coalesced. Justin abridging Trogus; Tertullian on
          Carthaginian religion four centuries later.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Stance: how does this source treat the claim?</h2>
      <p>
        On every claim, each cited source carries a stance: does the source
        <em>support</em>, <em>qualify</em>, or <em>contradict</em> the claim?
        The site uses a small inline glyph:
      </p>
      <ul class="not-prose space-y-2 my-6 text-sm">
        <li class="flex items-baseline gap-3">
          <span class="text-emerald-700"><span class="font-bold">✓</span> supports</span>
          <span class="text-sand-700">— the source affirms the claim.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-baseline gap-3">
          <span class="text-amber-700"><span class="font-bold">~</span> qualifies</span>
          <span class="text-sand-700">— the source partially supports the claim, or supports it under conditions, or gives a different number / detail than another source.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-baseline gap-3">
          <span class="text-rose-700"><span class="font-bold">✗</span> contradicts</span>
          <span class="text-sand-700">— the source actively disagrees with the claim, and is cited so the reader can see the disagreement.</span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Contradicting sources are surfaced rather than hidden. The point is
        not to tidy the evidence into agreement; the point is to show the
        reader where the sources actually pull against each other.
      </p>

      <h2>Editorial takes: when the site argues</h2>
      <p>
        Some questions the surviving sources don't directly settle, and a
        synthesis is unavoidable. For those, the site occasionally posts an
        <strong>editorial take</strong> — a labeled, signed position with
        explicit reasoning, the competing positions named and engaged, and
        a confidence chip in a different vocabulary:
      </p>
      <ul class="not-prose space-y-3 my-6">
        <li class="flex items-baseline gap-3 flex-wrap">
          <EditorialConfidenceChip confidence="tentative" />
          <span class="flex-1">A working position, held lightly. We would not be surprised to revise it.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-baseline gap-3 flex-wrap">
          <EditorialConfidenceChip confidence="moderate" />
          <span class="flex-1">A position held with some confidence — revisable, but not lightly.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-baseline gap-3 flex-wrap">
          <EditorialConfidenceChip confidence="strong" />
          <span class="flex-1">A position held with high confidence, given the evidence currently available.</span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Editorial takes are visually marked, never blended into surrounding
        prose, and always name the readings they're rejecting and why.
        Strong takes are fine; quiet takes hidden inside paragraphs are not.
      </p>

      <h2>Open questions: what we don't know</h2>
      <p>
        Where the evidence does not support a confident answer in any
        direction, the gap itself gets a page. Open-question pages name
        the question, explain why it's unknown, summarize the candidate
        answers and the arguments for each, and describe what would
        actually resolve the question if it were ever to come — a new
        inscription, a securely dated stratum, a recovered text.
      </p>
      <p>
        Treating gaps as first-class entities is the site's protest against
        the encyclopedia habit of pretending the evidence is denser than it
        is. The structural question of Carthaginian internal politics
        between roughly 480 and 264 BCE is a hole in the record, and the
        right thing to do is name it.
      </p>

      <h2>Narratives, themes, periods: when prose is the right shape</h2>
      <p>
        Atomic claims are the right shape for facts. They are the wrong
        shape for arcs. The Tophet controversy is a two-century debate
        across multiple disciplines; reducing it to a list of claim cards
        loses the shape of the disagreement. So the site also has
        long-form prose — <strong>narratives</strong> for biographical or
        debate arcs, <strong>themes</strong> for cross-cutting topics like
        Punic religion or Carthaginian governance, <strong>periods</strong>
        for era-level synthesis.
      </p>
      <p>
        The convention there is the same as everywhere else: positions are
        attributed, citations are inline (Polybius 3.22, Plutarch <em>Cato
        Major</em> 27 — auto-linked to the source page), and where the
        prose takes a strong reading, the competing reading is named.
      </p>

      <h2>Texts, editions, and translations</h2>
      <p>
        Ancient works are cited by their standard internal divisions
        (Polybius 3.22, Livy 21.4, Diodorus 20.14), which are stable
        across editions and translations; a citation here can be checked
        against any scholarly text of the work. Passage summaries are the
        site's own paraphrases, worked from the public-domain translations
        named on each source page (Shuckburgh's Polybius, for example) and
        checked against the modern literature where the rendering of a
        passage is itself disputed.
      </p>
      <p>
        Where a standard scholarly apparatus exists for a text, the source
        page says so — Walbank's <em>Historical Commentary</em> for
        Polybius is the model case. Punic and Phoenician inscriptions are
        cited by corpus number (CIS, KAI), the convention the epigraphic
        literature uses.
      </p>

      <h2>How this site is produced</h2>
      <p>
        The site is written and maintained by one editor, working with AI
        assistance (Claude) for drafting, cross-referencing, and
        consistency checking. The scope, the positions taken, the
        confidence labels, and the final review of every page are
        editorial decisions, and the responsibility for errors is the
        editor's, not the tool's. The apparatus described on this page —
        claims tied to cited passages, confidence labels, named competing
        readings — exists precisely so that readers can check the work
        against the sources rather than extend trust to the process that
        produced it.
      </p>

      <h2>Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        Errors, miscitations, and out-of-date readings can be flagged
        through the <a href="/about">contact form</a>. Corrections are
        made in place: the page's "last revised" stamp is updated, and
        where a correction changes a position rather than a detail, the
        page says so rather than silently swapping readings. On a source
        base this thin, being correctable is worth more than appearing
        infallible.
      </p>

      <h2>What this site does not do</h2>
      <p>
        It does not paraphrase translations as if they were the original
        text — passage summaries are the site's own paraphrase, with
        public-domain quotes reserved for places where the exact wording
        matters. It does not flatten contested questions into smooth
        summaries. It does not pretend the source base is denser than it
        is. It does not use authority where confidence labels would do the
        same work more honestly.
      </p>
      <p>
        Where the site is wrong — and on a topic this thinly sourced, it
        will sometimes be wrong — the structure is designed to make the
        wrongness fixable: each claim is a single file, each source is a
        single page, each take has a date and an open-to-revision flag.
        Corrections are welcome.
      </p>

      <hr class="my-10 border-tyrian-500" />
      <p class="text-sm text-sand-700">
        This methodology page is itself revisable. If a convention here
        does not match what you see across the site, the convention is
        wrong (or out of date) and worth flagging.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
